I'm a Rust newbie trying to figure out the language by playing with it. I've hit some problems on trying to return an array from a function:

struct Widget {
  thingies: ~[int]
}

impl Widget {
    fn new() -> Widget {
        Widget { thingies: ~[4, 8, 15, 16, 23, 42] }
    }

    fn somethings(&self) -> ~[int] {
        self.thingies
    }
}

fn main() {
   let widget = Widget::new();
   let wotsits = widget.somethings();
}

This of course fails compilation with this error:

pointers.rs:11:8: 11:21 error: cannot move out of dereference of & pointer
pointers.rs:11         self.thingies

In case this code sample looks out of sorts, all I'm trying to do is pull an array out of an implemented struct. The borrowed pointer isn't important, it's just how I'm trying to store the data.

Any tips on how to extract my array properly?

Btw, I'm using Rust 0.8

In Rust, you cannot move out of a borrowed pointer. To return an array from a function, you have a few options:

1. Return an owned array:

```rust
fn somethings(&self) -> ~[int] {
    ~self.thingies 
}
```

This will return an owned copy of the array, allowing the caller to take ownership of it.

2. Return a Vec<T> (vector) instead of a raw array:

```rust
fn somethings(&self) -> Vec<int> {
    self.thingies.to_vec()
} 
```

This will return an owned vector containing the values. Vectors have more functionality than arrays, so this is often preferable.

3. Change the function to take self by value instead of by reference:

```rust
fn somethings(self) -> ~[int] {
    self.thingies
}
```

Now the function takes ownership of self, and can return the inner array. The downside is that self is consumed by the function call.

4. Return a reference instead of an owned value:

```rust 
fn somethings(&self) -> &[~int] {
    &self.thingies
}
```

This returns a reference to the internal array. The caller can then use that reference, but does not own the underlying data.

In your specific example, I would recommend option 2 - returning a Vec<int>:

```rust
impl Widget {
    fn new() -> Widget { ... }

    fn somethings(&self) -> Vec<int> {
        self.thingies.to_vec()
    }
}

fn main() {
   let widget = Widget::new();
   let wotsits = widget.somethings(); 
   // `wotsits` is a `Vec<int>` that we own
}
```

Hope this helps clarify array/vector returns in Rust! Let me know if you have any other questions.

